00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:51.600
ZACK AKIL: Yes.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:52.770
Thank you very much, Jon.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.860
I am a machine learning
engineer and developer advocate

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.450
for the Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.940
JON FAUST: And what
specifically do you work on?

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.520
ZACK AKIL: So my
role and projects

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.590
are all focused around
trying to inspire

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.670
developers to implement machine
learning into their projects.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.920
GABI FERRARA: What is a
favorite machine learning tool

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.710
that you have on Google Cloud
Platform that you recommend

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:14.850
that you like most?

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.459
ZACK AKIL: The products
that I focus around are

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:20.370
all the machine learning
APIs and the machine

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.890
learning AutoMLs, which we
like to call the building

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:26.400
blocks of machine learning, that
allow you to gain some power

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.160
and value from your data
without necessarily needing

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.560
to know all the in-depth
knowledge around machine

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.238
learning.

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.030
JON FAUST: Awesome.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:37.020
So, we've mentioned a lot
of the AI and ML tools

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.660
and AutoML tools in our like
"Cool Things of the Week"

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:40.835
as they do product updates.

00:04:40.835 --> 00:04:42.990
So, can you give
us your definition

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.110
of AutoML Vision, AutoML
Video, and best practices,

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:47.692
and maybe what
they're best used for?

00:04:47.692 --> 00:04:49.150
ZACK AKIL: The most
common use case

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.050
that I always tell people
about is if anyone's ever

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.300
used Google Photos
on their phone

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.730
or on their Pixel, where
they're able to search

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.400
through their own images
based off of tags which they

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.560
themselves didn't put
in-- so if you've taken

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.320
a picture of the beach, you can
search for the word "beach,"

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.800
and it'll find from your own
photos pictures of beaches.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.480
And that's actually
a feature that's

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.860
built into the Vision API.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:14.220
So, you can upload any image,
and it will automatically

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.070
annotate it for you with objects
that are inside the image.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.230
And it can even
do things like OCR

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.480
or text recognition
within the image.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.420
And we commonly use
that for if people just

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.077
have a large storage
of images or videos

00:05:27.077 --> 00:05:29.160
to automatically annotate
them to make them easier

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:32.190
to search through or to
gain some insight about what

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.170
videos or pictures
people are uploading

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:36.080
into certain surfaces.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.540
GABI FERRARA: That explains why
when we look at Google Photos

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:44.400
and if I search for "winter,"
I can see snow or hoodies,

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.340
me with hoodies,
or with a beanie.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:48.570
Is that correct?

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.460
ZACK AKIL: Yeah,
that's the kind of tags

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:52.470
which will appear when you
search those sort of things.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:53.310
JON FAUST: Awesome.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.800
And that also explains
why it's so easy for me

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.440
to find a lot of my game images
that I take screenshots of.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:01.080
Fully.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.280
And hopefully try to recreate
later on when I'm practicing my

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:05.435
[INAUDIBLE].

00:06:05.435 --> 00:06:06.570
ZACK AKIL: Exactly, yeah.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:08.490
GABI FERRARA: With
AutoML Vision,

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:10.020
what is the most
common scenario?

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.463
Is it streaming or other kind
of annotation like AutoML Image?

00:06:14.463 --> 00:06:16.380
ZACK AKIL: One of the
main use cases for Video

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:19.230
is in video archive and
search, because a lot

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:21.360
of times with companies
especially, companies

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.340
that maybe have their
own storage clients

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.270
or are storing a lot of videos--

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:27.090
things like media companies--

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.958
they want to store their
videos and search through them

00:06:29.958 --> 00:06:32.250
quickly without having to go
through and annotate them.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:35.190
Common use case that someone
came to me with recently

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.470
was with regards
to video editing.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:42.653
So especially if people are
recording niche video scenarios

00:06:42.653 --> 00:06:44.070
where they're
interviewing people,

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.620
and they maybe want to get all
the videos where people are

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:50.550
laughing, that's not a feature
that is built into any API

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:51.340
at the moment.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:55.990
But it is a feature that can
be trained using AutoML Video.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:58.780
So, if you want a
special type of detection

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.350
which detects when people
are laughing, or maybe

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.450
special camera
movements, then you

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:05.280
can train your own
AutoML to detect those

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.925
so they will automatically
retrieve them when you want to.

00:07:07.925 --> 00:07:09.060
JON FAUST: Awesome.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.598
So, you really focus on
AutoML Vision and Video.

00:07:11.598 --> 00:07:13.890
So when I first met you,
Zack, maybe earlier this year,

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:15.348
you had a demo
running on your desk

00:07:15.348 --> 00:07:17.575
that if you placed an
item in front of a camera,

00:07:17.575 --> 00:07:19.950
would tell you whether or not
the actual item was healthy

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:20.520
or not.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.020
And I think the
sample data that you

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.300
were using fruits and candy.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.175
So, it seems like you
have a maker's approach

00:07:26.175 --> 00:07:28.342
for all of the work that
you do and all of the demos

00:07:28.342 --> 00:07:28.930
that you do.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:30.810
So can you tell us a
little bit about how

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.018
you come up with those ideas?

00:07:32.018 --> 00:07:33.000
ZACK AKIL: Absolutely.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.860
It all goes back to when
I first got into tech.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.220
And it was I got
an Arduino, which

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.040
was this small,
prototyping electronics

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.200
board for making projects to
make LED and buttons light up.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.120
And that was my first
intro into programming.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.870
And so when I started
learning using that,

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:53.250
I find it a lot more rewarding
seeing even just a single LED

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:56.420
light up compared to having
some text on the screen move

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:57.670
in a different way.

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:59.460
So since then, I fell
in love with making

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:02.520
physical things happen,
physical hardware demos.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.330
And then after university, I
looked into machine learning.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:10.020
And it was a lot of really cool
machine learning applications

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:12.270
that were running on
UIs, and desktops,

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.620
and exporting CSV files.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.240
And I really wanted to combine
my newfound interest of machine

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:23.220
learning with my old passion
of hardware and electronics.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:25.870
So now when I come
up with ideas,

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:28.080
I always try to combine
machine learning and embedded

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.330
technologies, just because
I think machine learning is

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.940
one of the most interesting
new technologies

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:34.890
that people are getting into.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:38.070
And hardware electronics
is one of the most fun ways

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.088
to interact with any
kind of technology.

00:08:40.088 --> 00:08:41.755
So I just always try
to combine the two.

00:08:41.755 --> 00:08:43.950
GABI FERRARA: And you
also created a demo

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:47.820
that I think you won a
prize or something about it

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:49.830
with a bike in ML?

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:51.373
Can you tell us
about more of that?

00:08:51.373 --> 00:08:52.290
ZACK AKIL: Absolutely.

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:55.320
So one of my more
recent projects

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:59.190
is a smart AI-powered
bicycle, which

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:02.490
performs real-time image
classification of what's

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.530
happening behind the cyclist.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.870
So as you're cycling
down the road, and say,

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.420
a large truck is
approaching from behind,

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:13.200
your handlebars will light up
red to indicate there's danger.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.558
And it will light up
on maybe the right hand

00:09:15.558 --> 00:09:17.100
side of your handlebars
if your truck

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.183
is on the right-hand side.

00:09:18.183 --> 00:09:21.100
And if the truck pulls
from the right to the left,

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.310
you'll see a kind
of blob of LEDs move

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:27.180
across your handlebars
to represent the truck.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.870
And I built this
using the Coral Edge

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.150
TPU, which is one
of Google's products

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.090
for running machine learning
fast on embedded systems.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:37.980
And that plugs into
a Raspberry Pi.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:41.550
And I built this, and I built
it onto a physical bike.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:44.970
And we recently had
an internal hackathon.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:48.330
And in that hackathon, it
won first prize as just

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:52.710
like a hacky project and
quirky implementation

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:53.585
of machine learning.

00:09:53.585 --> 00:09:54.960
But it's actually
a project which

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.760
I plan to continue
working on until I

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.770
can get a more solid working
concept going and potentially

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.710
working with local buy
companies in London

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.626
to see if we can get it
onto real bikes for people.

00:10:07.626 --> 00:10:08.960
JON FAUST: That's impressive.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:11.400
I would assume that
you're a cyclist yourself.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.937
And there's probably some
real life inspiration

00:10:13.937 --> 00:10:14.770
behind this project.

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.062
ZACK AKIL: Oh yeah, absolutely.

00:10:16.062 --> 00:10:19.390
There's some horror stories
around being heckled

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:23.310
at on the road by accidentally
pulling in front of people

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:24.850
without seeing
that they're there,

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:27.667
but knowing that I couldn't
really do anything because when

00:10:27.667 --> 00:10:28.750
you're cycling in London--

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:32.350
I'm sure it's similar for other
busy cities like New York--

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:35.950
you really can't turn around to
check because you're constantly

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:38.320
having to look forward.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:39.850
It's just like an
extreme sport is

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:41.200
the way I always classify it.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:44.140
And the current
technology to help

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.390
people be more aware on
the road hasn't really

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.100
gotten past mirrors.

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:50.258
They're ugly, and they
don't run machine learning.

00:10:50.258 --> 00:10:51.675
So that's why I
came up with this.

00:10:51.675 --> 00:10:52.758
JON FAUST: That's awesome.

00:10:52.758 --> 00:10:55.420
It's kind of funny, because in
New York you get the opposite.

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.490
It's the cyclists who
heckle you when you're

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.438
driving throughout the city.

00:10:59.438 --> 00:11:01.980
ZACK AKIL: The funny thing is
when I took this, I built this,

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.210
and I built a travel
version of this bike,

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:07.630
so like a flat pack
IKEA model of a bicycle

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:09.220
that I would take
around and assemble

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.020
on stage to show people.

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:14.560
And I took it down to Sydney,
and I showed it to people.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.770
They all loved it because
in Sydney a similar problem.

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:23.350
But they also notified me of
a specific regional problem

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:26.140
to Australia, which
is for cyclists,

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.590
the biggest danger isn't
necessarily trucks or cars,

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:31.930
but actually
territorial birds that

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.760
will sweep down on cyclists.

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.085
[MAN SCREAMING]

00:11:36.085 --> 00:11:36.820
[CRASHING SOUND]

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:37.320
Yeah.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:40.180
And it's a very, very
common issue in Australia.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:43.750
And in fact, it's very common
to see cyclists cycle around

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:45.970
with zip ties tied
to their helmets

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:50.400
to create spiky hair to scare
away birds from attacking them.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:52.960
And, yeah, it's a crazy problem.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:55.480
And the initial
version of the bike

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:58.420
was just using an
off-the-shelf model

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:00.550
that had traffic detection.

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:04.120
But I was thinking of
making an Australian version

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:08.020
of the bike that has a model
trained in AutoML that has

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:12.437
both traffic and territorial
birds so that it can detect,

00:12:12.437 --> 00:12:14.520
oh, there's a car, there's
a car, there's a truck,

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:15.930
and there's a
falcon coming back!

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:17.180
[RECORDING OF PEOPLE LAUGHING]

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:18.820
And what's cool is
with AutoML, it's

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:20.945
actually quite straightforward
because AutoML Noise

00:12:20.945 --> 00:12:24.040
supports exporting object
detection models to the Coral

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:24.705
Edge TPU.

00:12:24.705 --> 00:12:25.960
GABI FERRARA: Oh!

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:29.440
So, you have it on your
hand without having

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:32.410
to connect to the
internet or relying on 4G?

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:34.240
ZACK AKIL: Exactly, so, yeah.

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:37.000
You can take a model that's
trained in the cloud on AutoML

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.000
and just download it to
run locally on a device.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.680
So, you don't need a connection
to the internet for it to work.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:44.120
GABI FERRARA: And about that--

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:47.497
you also presented that
demo on Cloud Next London.

00:12:47.497 --> 00:12:48.580
ZACK AKIL: Yes, yes I did.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:50.080
GABI FERRARA: How
was the reception?

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:52.810
ZACK AKIL: I always start the
talk by asking, who in the room

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:54.190
is a cyclist?

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:57.280
Fortunately, it seems a lot of
people in tech like cycling,

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:00.240
because it's always the entire
room that raises their hand.

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:01.730
So they seemed to
really like it.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:04.720
I always also ask, OK,
who cycles in London?

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:06.700
Usually it's about the
same number of people--

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:08.860
if the talk's in
London, of course.

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:10.810
And then I'll follow
that up with, OK,

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:14.710
who would recommend cycling
in London to a family member?

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:16.810
And then all the hands go down.

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:19.210
So everyone's on the
same page as like,

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:20.260
we all cycle in London.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:22.790
And we all think it's
very, very dangerous.

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:25.500
We wouldn't want
anyone to do it.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:26.890
I show them the bike.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:28.060
They seem to really like it.

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:30.050
And then people always
come up afterwards.

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:32.050
Usually actually every
time I've given the talk,

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:33.967
there will be someone
that comes up afterwards

00:13:33.967 --> 00:13:37.080
who has an injury that they
got that week from cycling

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:37.580
in London.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:40.615
And they'd be like, I really
could've used this this week!

00:13:40.615 --> 00:13:42.490
So, it really resonates
with a lot of people.

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:43.180
GABI FERRARA: Except the birds.

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:43.460
[BIRDS CAWING]

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:44.127
ZACK AKIL: Yeah.

00:13:44.127 --> 00:13:46.115
JON FAUST: That's awesome.

00:13:46.115 --> 00:13:48.580
[JON LAUGHING]

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:50.080
And then I do
remember when you were

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:52.540
in New York you were working
on that image classification

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:53.297
matrix or something like that?

00:13:53.297 --> 00:13:54.047
ZACK AKIL: Oh, OK.

00:13:54.047 --> 00:13:55.615
I was doing a
little side project.

00:13:55.615 --> 00:13:56.310
JON FAUST: Yes

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:58.143
ZACK AKIL: And it was
actually my first time

00:13:58.143 --> 00:14:01.270
doing image generation where
you can get an AI to generate

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:02.630
new images for you.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:07.000
And I actually-- I used
TensorFlow.js on my personal

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:10.220
website ZackAkil.com
to generate--

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:11.726
[EVERYONE CHUCKLES]

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:15.227
--to generate the actual title
of the website in real time.

00:14:15.227 --> 00:14:16.810
So as you're moving
your mouse around,

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:17.925
it's generating the title.

00:14:17.925 --> 00:14:18.925
It's completely useless.

00:14:18.925 --> 00:14:19.180
[RECORDING OF PEOPLE LAUGHING]

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:20.458
It doesn't solve any problems.

00:14:20.458 --> 00:14:22.000
It's just-- it was
just a bit of fun.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.820
That's how I have fun is
to experiment with new AI.

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:27.670
GABI FERRARA: Beyond the
demos that you build,

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:29.590
I think you have a
lot of interactions

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:32.680
with other developers, too,
being a developer advocate.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:37.900
Did someone else come to you
with a really cool, interesting

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:42.190
implementation from AutoML
DML APIs beyond that one

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:44.690
that you mentioned
about the laughing one?

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:47.305
Beyond that, do you
have any other examples?

00:14:47.305 --> 00:14:49.065
ZACK AKIL: One of the
Next San Franciscos,

00:14:49.065 --> 00:14:51.430
and there was a [INAUDIBLE]
talk about giving a video,

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.260
and using the Video
Intelligence API,

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:57.070
and the Vision API
automatically bleep out anytime

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:58.750
a person says an acronym.

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:01.570
And immediately afterwards,
someone came up to me

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:04.660
and said that they wanted to
use this kind of technology

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:08.800
using an AutoML Video model to
detect when someone is talking

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:10.990
about a specific subject.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:14.020
At the time I think it
was "Game of Thrones"

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:16.390
was still pretty hot.

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:20.130
And they wanted to
detect when, say, there

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:22.060
was a video of someone
being interviewed,

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:25.070
and they gave away a spoiler
to "Game of Thrones" and beep

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:26.485
out when they say the spoiler.

00:15:26.485 --> 00:15:27.700
ELECTRONIC MALE VOICE:
Hang on a minute.

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:30.030
I thought Ned Stark was
the main character and then

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.030
they chopped off [BLEEP]

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:33.160
ZACK AKIL: So, they were going
to use AutoML to build a custom

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:34.320
model to detect that.

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:35.480
So, I thought that
was pretty cool.

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:37.330
GABI FERRARA: They should
did that for trailers, too.

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:37.967
I can't stand--

00:15:37.967 --> 00:15:38.800
ZACK AKIL: Oh, yeah.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:39.820
GABI FERRARA: --when
you go somewhere--

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:42.130
I don't watch trailers
because it spoils it for me.

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:43.300
They reveal everything.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.405
And I want that everywhere.

00:15:45.405 --> 00:15:46.780
ZACK AKIL: That
would be perfect.

00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:49.480
I feel like it's going crazy
now with the amount of trailers.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:51.190
They just give away
the whole film.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:54.070
Perfect use case for an
AutoML Video detection model.

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:56.080
So, automatically
trim down the trailer

00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:57.790
to just keep things interesting.

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:00.940
JON FAUST: So Zack, I know
that you're quite the athlete.

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:03.520
I know that you play rugby.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:04.420
ZACK AKIL: Yes.

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:05.740
JON FAUST: When you
came to visit New York,

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:07.905
you just randomly decided
to go to pick up dodgeball?

00:16:07.905 --> 00:16:09.700
ZACK AKIL: I tried to find
rugby, but they didn't have it.

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:10.870
But I settled for dodgeball.

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:11.710
[JON LAUGHING]

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:15.340
JON FAUST: So, do you see
yourself using ML in the sports

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:17.400
and recreational
activities realm?

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:18.880
Or can you tell us
about some demos

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:20.172
that you've done in that space?

00:16:20.172 --> 00:16:21.520
ZACK AKIL: Absolutely.

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:25.450
That was my original
inspiration to get

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:27.220
into machine
learning was actually

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:30.970
to build automated camera
equipment for filming

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:33.070
grassroots rugby matches.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:35.800
Because at school level, I
didn't play rugby initially,

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:37.002
but I recorded the matches.

00:16:37.002 --> 00:16:38.710
And then when I started
playing, I really

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:40.150
wanted people to
record my matches.

00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:42.067
But nobody wanted to do
it because they didn't

00:16:42.067 --> 00:16:45.330
want to stand out there in the
Irish freezing weather holding

00:16:45.330 --> 00:16:46.180
a camera steady.

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:48.220
One of my first ever
physical projects

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:51.160
was actually a camera that
would automatically follow

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:52.660
rugby matches and record it.

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:55.450
And I think I gave my first
ever talk at a Pi data

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:57.070
conference about that demo.

00:16:57.070 --> 00:17:01.990
And anytime AutoML releases a
new feature, my first examples

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:05.829
of demos are always to do
a sport or sport tracking.

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:09.099
So I'm actually already
talking with some

00:17:09.099 --> 00:17:13.089
of the lead coaches of the
Great Britain Tag Rugby

00:17:13.089 --> 00:17:17.319
teams about using AutoML Video
tracking and things like that

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:19.329
in combination with drone
footage that's already

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:21.700
been taken of training
sessions to try

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:24.940
to say, track players, and
track their acceleration,

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:26.478
and things like that.

00:17:26.478 --> 00:17:27.520
And so, yeah, absolutely.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.430
I expect to see quite a few
sport-related demos, especially

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:31.070
in summertime.

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:32.320
Not so much in the wintertime.

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:33.505
It's too cold.

00:17:33.505 --> 00:17:35.410
GABI FERRARA: Except
if it's curling.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:37.210
You could do something
for curling, too.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:38.400
ZACK AKIL: Ooh!

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:41.850
That could be nice,
tracking the curl and stuff.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:43.420
It's not big over here.

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:43.935
It hasn't--

00:17:43.935 --> 00:17:46.060
GABI FERRARA: I just-- like
you said, winter sport.

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:47.630
And that one came to my mind.

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:48.765
ZACK AKIL: Winter sport.

00:17:48.765 --> 00:17:50.370
GABI FERRARA: So
what is the demo

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:51.690
that you're working on now?

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.930
ZACK AKIL: Well, we're just
finishing up all our work

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:56.970
on our most recent
demo, which was

00:17:56.970 --> 00:18:01.480
in partnership with the Football
Association here in the UK.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:04.800
And it was actually the
main demo of the Next London

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:08.220
Conference, which involved
three camera phones--

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:10.320
so their actual Pixel 4 phones--

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:14.760
to record a penalty kick from
a full-siz football, or soccer

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:15.990
net.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:18.180
And it would upload
all the video footage

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.970
to the cloud using
Firestore and analyze them

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:22.860
with video intelligence
and all this stuff,

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:25.450
so that a person would go up,
take three kicks, and then they

00:18:25.450 --> 00:18:27.900
would immediately see a
dashboard with the speed

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:30.780
and accuracy along with
the style of their kick,

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:33.680
and get their own player
profile card printed out

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:34.950
for their sticker book.

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:36.200
JON FAUST: Oh, that's awesome.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:38.725
GABI FERRARA: How long was the
delay from the kick to having

00:18:38.725 --> 00:18:39.225
the card?

00:18:39.225 --> 00:18:41.760
ZACK AKIL: From kick to
card, the actual printer

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.800
itself took a minute.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:46.200
Getting the kick with
both the actual power

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:51.810
and the style calculations
took about 15 seconds

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:53.370
conservatively.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:55.770
Sometimes a person would
forget to hit "stop recording,"

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:57.675
and it would take half a minute.

00:18:57.675 --> 00:19:00.300
But around 15 seconds
to get all of the data,

00:19:00.300 --> 00:19:03.240
continuously analyze,
and then into that image

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:04.568
is about that 15 seconds.

00:19:04.568 --> 00:19:05.610
GABI FERRARA: Impressive.

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:06.880
JON FAUST: It's pretty cool.

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:08.630
So, I'm kind of curious,
what ML tools are

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:11.058
you using when it comes
to following trajectories?

00:19:11.058 --> 00:19:12.600
Because I would
assume that if you're

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:14.880
bending a ball under
a shot, then there

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:16.920
has to be some way of
tracking the trajectory,

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:21.180
and then hopefully coming up
with a more precise calculation

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:23.640
on, I guess you can
call it, the style.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.730
ZACK AKIL: The way we calculated
the accuracy-- this is probably

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:29.520
the bit of the project that
I was most proud of doing.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:33.780
Because what we did is we
used AutoML Object Detection,

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:36.690
and we just wanted initially
to track a football moving

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:38.340
across the screen.

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:41.340
And there's already
lots of public data sets

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:44.800
that have footballs and
other sports balls labeled.

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:47.762
And we actually used
the public COCO dataset,

00:19:47.762 --> 00:19:48.720
which you can download.

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:51.600
And I actually took that and
dumped it into AutoML Object

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:55.080
Detection, scraping out
just the photos of balls,

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:58.600
and then trained a model that
would track the soccer balls.

00:19:58.600 --> 00:20:00.930
And that was the initial model.

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:05.750
It performed all right until
the balls were kicked too fast,

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:08.190
because then they stopped
looking like sharp soccer balls

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.153
and were just blurry
smudges across the screen.

00:20:10.153 --> 00:20:12.570
And that's when we actually
just collected a bit more data

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:15.870
and labeled it in the AutoML
UI, which is a good use

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:18.060
case for slightly more
customized models,

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.240
because the public data set
didn't have blurry soccer

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:22.340
balls.

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:25.740
And after that, it was
performing 99% percent accuracy

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:27.780
on tracking these
fast-moving objects.

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:29.820
And so then what
we did to calculate

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:33.300
accuracy is we just recorded
someone taking a kick,

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:36.450
for each frame, detect
where the ball is.

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:39.040
And then-- I had a bit of
a data science background,

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:42.660
so I played around with
visualizing maybe, OK,

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:45.070
what's the speed of the
ball moving in pixels,

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:48.145
and what's the trajectory of
the ball moving in pixels?

00:20:48.145 --> 00:20:49.770
And eventually, I
stumbled across a way

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:52.140
of detecting when the
ball actually made impact

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:54.600
with the goal by
continuously checking

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:56.850
the direction of the
ball that is moving,

00:20:56.850 --> 00:21:00.540
and then as soon as the
direction changed suddenly,

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:02.890
that was when it had an
impact with something.

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.500
And I would just take that
as the point of impact.

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:07.890
And then we were
also using AutoML

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:12.747
to track the targets that we
had hanging in the goal net.

00:21:12.747 --> 00:21:14.580
And then once we knew
the impact of the ball

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:16.247
and the position of
the targets, we just

00:21:16.247 --> 00:21:19.410
did a little distance metric
to calculate accuracy.

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:20.680
JON FAUST: Wow.

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:22.680
That's really cool that
you found a work around,

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:26.505
because I know that when
it comes to sports imaging,

00:21:26.505 --> 00:21:29.130
they typically use cameras that
have really high refresh rates.

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:31.740
That way you can capture
images really quickly.

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:34.050
But knowing that ML
can solve that issue

00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:35.290
with just your standard--

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:36.870
I believe you said you
used Pixel 4 phones, right?

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:38.537
ZACK AKIL: We used
Pixel 4 phones, yeah,

00:21:38.537 --> 00:21:41.190
because actually when we were
building the initial prototype,

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:43.730
we were using my
own Pixel 2 phone.

00:21:43.730 --> 00:21:47.070
And because most Android phones
have a slow motion capture

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:51.540
of 120 frames minimum, that was
fast enough to capture-- even

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:54.798
people were really
hammering these kicks,

00:21:54.798 --> 00:21:56.340
and it was tracking
them, no problem.

00:21:56.340 --> 00:21:57.210
JON FAUST: That's
pretty awesome.

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:58.320
ZACK AKIL: That's
using machine learning

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:00.300
to do the heavy lifting
rather than getting

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:01.780
more high-end technology.

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:03.460
I think the original
spec of the demo

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:07.260
we were going to go for these
high-end, high-speed cameras.

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:11.380
But my kind of hackery
nature wouldn't allow it.

00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:13.800
I was like, no, I just
want to use a mobile phone

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:15.562
because it's simpler,
and more people

00:22:15.562 --> 00:22:16.770
have access to mobile phones.

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:19.945
And rather than having a model
that detects a sharp soccer

00:22:19.945 --> 00:22:22.320
ball, we'll just train a model
that detects a blurry one.

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:23.093
It's fine.

00:22:23.093 --> 00:22:25.010
JON FAUST: Way to think
