00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:48.240
JON FAUST: So, our
question of the week

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:51.590
is how to run cloud functions
in a local environment.

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:52.090
So, Gabi?

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:56.280
GABI FERRARA: The cool thing
about serverless architecture

00:24:56.280 --> 00:25:00.010
is that you don't need to
worry about infrastructure.

00:25:00.010 --> 00:25:02.850
But sometimes you
want to test something

00:25:02.850 --> 00:25:06.040
without deploying first,
which is good practice.

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:09.090
So, you can now use
Google Cloud Functions

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:12.870
for a framework, which it is an
extension for Node.js where you

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:16.440
can create a cloud
function locally,

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:21.270
and you serve your Node.js
application using Express.

00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:25.000
And you can hit the URL
that you're just serving

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:26.880
and test your cloud
function locally

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:29.340
without having to
connect necessarily

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:30.810
through the internet.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:34.080
So it makes your
testing easier, And you

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:36.870
don't have to wait to
deploy all the time that you

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:39.220
do a different
thing on a function.

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:41.148
So I think that's very useful.

00:25:41.148 --> 00:25:41.940
JON FAUST: Awesome.

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:44.697
Serverless without the internet.

00:25:44.697 --> 00:25:46.530
GABI FERRARA: Well,
it's not the same thing.

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:49.540
Well, it helps with
small case scenarios.

